{"term":"Scham","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"shame","concept":"sexual_inhibition","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":5,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The feeling of shame about one's body, sexuality, or sexual behavior; also (archaic/poetic) the genital or pubic region itself.","english_term":"shame","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"German Scham has a somatic double meaning absent in English: Schamhaare (pubic hair), Schamlippen (labia), Schambein (pubic bone). The shame-emotion and genital-anatomy senses are etymologically fused, giving the word a bodily weight that English 'shame' lacks."}
{"term":"Schande","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"disgrace","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Disgrace or scandal resulting from sexual transgression or moral failure; the public dishonor attached to a shameful sexual act.","english_term":"disgrace","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Stronger than Scham (subjective feeling); Schande is the social consequence — being publicly dishonored. 'Das ist eine Schande!' is the standard exclamation of moral outrage. Often frames the community's verdict rather than the offender's inner state."}
{"term":"Schandtat","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sexual_offense","concept":"indecent_act","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":35,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":78,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A shameful or outrageous deed, especially a sexual offense or act of moral depravity considered a violation of community standards.","english_term":"indecent act","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound: Schand- (shame/scandal) + Tat (act/deed). Often appears in criminal and ecclesiastical contexts for rape, sexual assault, or other acts that bring public disgrace. Slightly archaic in everyday use but still appears in media reporting of crimes."}
{"term":"Schamhaftigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"modesty","concept":"prudishness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"The quality of being naturally bashful or modest about sexual matters; an ingrained sense of sexual shame governing comportment.","english_term":"modesty","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Less pathological than Prüderie; implies cultivated or instilled sexual modesty rather than excessive neurotic inhibition. The -haftigkeit suffix marks it as a character trait. Fading from active everyday use; more common in literary and psychological discourse."}
{"term":"Prüderie","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"prudery","concept":"prudishness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"loanword","crude":10,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":88,"recognition_pct_peak":88,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Prudery; excessive or affected modesty about sexual matters, often implying hypocritical or pathological sexual inhibition.","english_term":"prudery","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Loanword via French 'pruderie'. Almost always used critically or ironically in modern German; the person is called 'prüde' (adjective). High recognition sustained by shared European cultural discourse about sexual openness vs. repression."}
{"term":"Sittenstrenge","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"moral_rigorism","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":5,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":72,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Moral rigorism; strictness or severity in upholding sexual and social standards of propriety.","english_term":"moral strictness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound: Sitten (morals/customs) + Strenge (strictness/severity). Associated with Wilhelmine-era and Victorian social conservatism in German-speaking countries. Somewhat dated; predominantly appears in historical and literary analysis rather than everyday speech."}
{"term":"Tugendhaftigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"virtue","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":0,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":70,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Virtuousness; the positive moral quality of being sexually pure, behaviorally restrained, and ethically upright.","english_term":"virtuousness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"The positive pole of the shame-morality axis; unlike Prüderie or Keuschheit carries no ironic loading. Contrasts directly with Lasterhaftigkeit. Somewhat literary and philosophical in contemporary usage; more common in religious or classical educational contexts."}
{"term":"Keuschheit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"chastity","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":0,"technical":25,"recognition_pct_current":90,"recognition_pct_peak":93,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Chastity; the sexual virtue of abstaining from intercourse outside of marriage, or from all sexual activity as a religious ideal.","english_term":"chastity","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Central concept in Catholic and Protestant sexual ethics; Keuschheitsgelübde = vow of chastity. Widely understood but associated primarily with religious rather than secular discourse in contemporary Germany. Appears in youth purity-pledge contexts and theological literature."}
{"term":"Enthaltsamkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"abstinence","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":0,"technical":30,"recognition_pct_current":80,"recognition_pct_peak":85,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sexual abstinence or general temperance; deliberate self-restraint from sexual activity for moral, religious, or health reasons.","english_term":"abstinence","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Slightly broader than Keuschheit; applies to abstinence from any appetite (alcohol, food, sex). The sexual application is dominant in moralistic discourse. Used in sex-education contexts for abstinence-only programs and in health messaging."}
{"term":"Enthaltung","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"abstinence_formal","concept":"sexual_abstinence","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":0,"technical":35,"recognition_pct_current":73,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Abstention or formal restraint; the deliberate act of withholding oneself from sexual activity, framed procedurally or administratively rather than as virtue.","english_term":"abstention","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"More formal and neutral than Enthaltsamkeit; primary modern use is political (Enthaltung bei der Abstimmung = abstention in a vote). Sexual application requires explicit context and tends toward legal or regulatory framing."}
{"term":"Züchtigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"modesty_chastity","concept":"prudishness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":52,"recognition_pct_peak":70,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Modesty, chasteness, and sexual decency; an archaic term for the propriety expected of sexually respectable persons, especially women.","english_term":"chasteness","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"From züchtig (see below); largely displaced by Keuschheit and Schamhaftigkeit in active use. Carries a gentle cultivated-virtue connotation rather than harsh religious enforcement. Recognition concentrated in older and educated cohorts; fading in younger generations."}
{"term":"Züchtig","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"chaste_adjective","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"archaic","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":55,"recognition_pct_peak":73,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Chaste, modest, or decorous; an archaic adjective for sexually proper, restrained, or well-behaved persons.","english_term":"chaste","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"In older German literary tradition applied especially to women; also appears in compound Zucht und Ordnung (discipline and order). Rarely used in contemporary speech outside deliberate archaism or irony. Shares root with züchtigen (to discipline/punish), revealing the coercive dimension of sexual modesty norms."}
{"term":"Anstand","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"decency","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Decency and propriety in behavior, including sexual comportment; the social standard of respectable and non-offensive conduct.","english_term":"decency","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Covers all propriety, not exclusively sexual; 'das gehört sich nicht' (that is indecent) is the everyday equivalent. Anstandsregeln = rules of decency. A culturally central concept in German-speaking societies; failing to observe Anstand is a serious social offense."}
{"term":"Anständigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"respectability","concept":"sexual_conservatism","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":0,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Respectability and decency; the quality of adhering to accepted sexual and social norms in behavior and reputation.","english_term":"respectability","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Abstract noun from anständig (decent); implies both outward behavior and inner moral character. Slightly more emphatic than Anstand; frequently used in moral evaluation of persons rather than individual acts."}
{"term":"Unanständigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"indecency","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":22,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":82,"recognition_pct_peak":87,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Indecency or impropriety; sexually offensive or socially unacceptable behavior or language that violates standards of public decency.","english_term":"indecency","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"Direct antonym of Anständigkeit. Used in both everyday and legal-moral registers; 'grobe Unanständigkeit' (gross indecency) echoes criminal law language. Can describe crude speech, offensive gestures, or explicitly sexual acts in public."}
{"term":"Unanständig","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"indecent","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":28,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":95,"recognition_pct_peak":96,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Indecent, improper, or dirty; an adjective widely used for anything sexually offensive, crude, or violating social decorum.","english_term":"indecent","english_translation_quality":"direct","notes":"High-frequency everyday word across all registers; children learn it early ('das ist unanständig!'). Covers a wide range from mildly rude (dirty joke) to explicitly sexual (public nudity, obscene gesture) depending on context."}
{"term":"Sünde","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sin","concept":"fornication","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":15,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":99,"recognition_pct_peak":99,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sin; in religious and moral discourse, any sexual transgression regarded as a violation of divine or natural law.","english_term":"sin","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Generic German word for sin; in sexual contexts prototypically denotes fornication, adultery, and other church-condemned acts. Also used hyperbolically in secular speech ('das ist eine Sünde!' = that's a crime/shame). Mapped to fornication as the prototypical religious sexual sin concept."}
{"term":"Sündhaft","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"sinful","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":20,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":87,"recognition_pct_peak":92,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Sinful; describing sexual behavior, desires, or persons regarded as morally transgressive or violating divine law.","english_term":"sinful","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Adjective form of Sünde. Notably used hyperbolically in secular speech for something intensely pleasurable but supposedly forbidden ('sündhaft lecker' = sinfully delicious, 'sündhaft teuer' = sinfully expensive). This ironic secular use substantially coexists with the serious moral-religious register."}
{"term":"Sündenbock","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"religious_moral_status","subcategory":"scapegoat","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"moral_religious","crude":10,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":97,"recognition_pct_peak":97,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Scapegoat; a person designated to bear collective blame or stigma for a community's sexual transgressions or moral failures.","english_term":"scapegoat","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Derived from the biblical sin-goat (Leviticus 16) driven into the wilderness bearing communal sin. In sexual discourse applied when one person — often a woman — is made to carry the group's sexual shame. Mapped to slut_shaming as the closest available registry concept for blame-assignment in a sexual moral context; the match is imperfect since Sündenbock is the target, not the act."}
{"term":"Lasterhaft","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"depraved","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":28,"technical":10,"recognition_pct_current":65,"recognition_pct_peak":80,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Vicious, depraved, or dissolute; describing a person or lifestyle marked by habitual indulgence in immoral or sexual excess.","english_term":"depraved","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Adjective of Laster (vice); implies a habitual, character-defining moral degradation rather than a single transgression. Somewhat literary and dated in active use; more common in historical and theological texts than contemporary speech."}
{"term":"Laster","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"archaic_literary","subcategory":"vice","concept":"debauchery","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":25,"technical":15,"recognition_pct_current":83,"recognition_pct_peak":90,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Vice or depravity; a habitual moral failing or indulgent engagement in pleasures considered sinful, especially those of a sexual nature.","english_term":"vice","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Standard German word for vice; productive in compounds: Lastermaul (foul-mouthed scandalmonger), Lasterhöhle (den of iniquity/vice den), Lotterleben (dissolute life). Sexual vices are the prototypical Laster in religious discourse; term remains well-known through these compounds even as standalone use declines."}
{"term":"Unsittlichkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"immorality","concept":"licentiousness","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":20,"technical":20,"recognition_pct_current":60,"recognition_pct_peak":75,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Immorality or indecency; the general quality of disregarding accepted sexual and social moral codes.","english_term":"immorality","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Formal compound: un- + Sittlichkeit (morality/ethics). Used in moral-philosophical, legal-historical, and ecclesiastical discourse. Less common in everyday speech than Unanständigkeit; recognition concentrated in educated and older cohorts."}
{"term":"Sittenwidrig","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"immoral_legal","concept":"indecent_act","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":42,"recognition_pct_current":57,"recognition_pct_peak":68,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Contrary to good morals; a legal-moral adjective for sexual conduct that violates community moral standards, equivalent to the Latin contra bonos mores.","english_term":"contra bonos mores","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Legal term anchored in German civil law: §138 BGB voids contracts that are sittenwidrig (contrary to public morality). In sexual contexts marks behavior as both legally actionable and morally unacceptable. Recognition concentrated in legal, academic, and older educated registers."}
{"term":"Sittenwidrigkeit","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"honor_shame_economy","subcategory":"moral_violation_legal","concept":"indecency","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"literary","crude":15,"technical":42,"recognition_pct_current":50,"recognition_pct_peak":62,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"Immorality or violation of public morals; the legal-philosophical noun for conduct that offends community moral standards, used especially in contract and family law to invalidate transactions.","english_term":"immorality (legal)","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Noun pair to sittenwidrig; central to German legal doctrine under §138 BGB. More technical and formal than Unsittlichkeit; recognition concentrated in legal professionals and older educated cohorts. In sexual contexts encompasses prostitution contracts, coerced marriages, and similar instruments historically voided on moral grounds."}
{"term":"Schandmaul","lang":"de","script":"latin","romanization":null,"category":"insults","subcategory":"scandalmonger","concept":"slut_shaming","is_novel_concept":false,"register":"slang_neutral","crude":48,"technical":5,"recognition_pct_current":63,"recognition_pct_peak":78,"peak_year":null,"regional":null,"definition":"A scandalmonger or foul-mouthed person who spreads sexual gossip, shameful insults, or morally defaming speech about others' sexuality.","english_term":"scandalmonger","english_translation_quality":"approximate","notes":"Compound: Schand- (shame/scandal) + Maul (crude: mouth/snout, always pejorative when applied to humans). The Maul component itself contributes significant crudeness. Applied to someone whose speech weaponizes sexual shame; the compound is moderately archaic but widely recognized. Mapped to slut_shaming as the closest blame-distribution concept in the registry."}
